out in relation to the issue is `__abs__` and `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs`. Since the issue is about `rewrite(abs)` calling `rewrite(Abs)`, we should add a `_eval_rewrite_as_abs` method to `Expr` to handle this.\n\nNext, let's search for any existing `_eval_rewrite_as_x` methods in other classes to see if there are any patterns or examples we can follow.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['_eval_rewrite_as_']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>'**/*.py'</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite\nsympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:dpll_int_repr\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:pl_true_int_repr\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:unit_propagate\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:unit_propagate_int_repr\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:dpll_int_repr\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:find_unit_clause_int_repr\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:find_pure_symbol_int_repr\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:find_unit_clause\nsympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:find_pure_symbol\nNo files found for file pattern ''**/*.py''. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"_eval_rewrite_as_\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1996 | def rewrite(self, *args, deep=True, **hints):\n1997 |         \"\"\"\n1998 |         Rewrite *self* using a defined rule.\n1999 | \n2000 |         Rewriting transforms an expression to another, which is mathematically\n2001 |         equivalent but structurally different. For example you can rewrite\n2002 |         trigonometric functions as complex exponentials or combinatorial\n2003 |         functions as gamma function.\n2004 | \n2005 |         This method takes a *pattern* and a *rule* as positional arguments.\n2006 |         *pattern* is optional parameter which defines the types of expressions\n2007 |         that will be transformed. If it is not passed, all possible expressions\n2008 |         will be rewritten. *rule* defines how the expression will be rewritten.\n2009 | \n2010 |         Parameters\n2011 |         ==========\n2012 | \n2013 |         args : Expr\n2014 |             A *rule*, or *pattern* and *rule*.\n2015 |             - *pattern* is a type or an iterable of types.\n2016 |             - *rule* can be any object.\n2017 | \n2018 |         deep : bool, optional\n2019 |             If ``True``, subexpressions are recursively transformed. Default is\n2020 |             ``True``.\n2021 | \n2022 |         Examples\n2023 |         ========\n2024 | \n2025 |         If *pattern* is unspecified, all possible expressions are transformed.\n2026 | \n2027 |         >>> from sympy import cos, sin, exp, I\n2028 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x\n2029 |         >>> expr = cos(x) + I*sin(x)\n2030 |         >>> expr.rewrite(exp)\n2031 |         exp(I*x)\n2032 | \n2033 |         Pattern can be a type or an iterable of types.\n2034 | \n2035 |         >>> expr.rewrite(sin, exp)\n2036 |         exp(I*x)/2 + cos(x) - exp(-I*x)/2\n2037 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos,], exp)\n2038 |         exp(I*x)/2 + I*sin(x) + exp(-I*x)/2\n2039 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos, sin], exp)\n2040 |         exp(I*x)\n2041 | \n2042 |         Rewriting behavior can be implemented by defining ``_eval_rewrite()``\n2043 |         method.\n2044 | \n2045 |         >>> from sympy import Expr, sqrt, pi\n2046 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2047 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite(self, rule, args, **hints):\n2048 |         ...         x, = args\n2049 |         ...         if rule == cos:\n2050 |         ...             return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2051 |         ...         if rule == sqrt:\n2052 |         ...             return sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2053 |         >>> MySin(MySin(x)).rewrite(cos)\n2054 |         cos(-cos(-x + pi/2) + pi/2)\n2055 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(sqrt)\n2056 |         sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2057 | \n2058 |         Defining ``_eval_rewrite_as_[...]()`` method is supported for backwards\n2059 |         compatibility reason. This may be removed in the future and using it is\n2060 |         discouraged.\n2061 | \n2062 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2063 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite_as_cos(self, *args, **hints):\n2064 |         ...         x, = args\n2065 |         ...         return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2066 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(cos)\n2067 |         cos(-x + pi/2)\n2068 | \n2069 |         \"\"\"\n2070 |         if not args:\n2071 |             return self\n2072 | \n2073 |         hints.update(deep=deep)\n2074 | \n2075 |         pattern = args[:-1]\n2076 |         rule = args[-1]\n2077 | \n2078 |         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n2079 |         if isinstance(rule, str):\n2080 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n2081 |         elif hasattr(rule, \"__name__\"):\n2082 |             # rule is class or function\n2083 |             clsname = rule.__name__\n2084 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2085 |         else:\n2086 |             # rule is instance\n2087 |             clsname = rule.__class__.__name__\n2088 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2089 | \n2090 |         if pattern:\n2091 |             if iterable(pattern[0]):\n2092 |                 pattern = pattern[0]\n2093 |             pattern = tuple(p for p in pattern if self.has(p))\n2094 |             if not pattern:\n2095 |                 return self\n2096 |         # hereafter, empty pattern is interpreted as all pattern.\n2097 | \n2098 |         return self._rewrite(pattern, rule, method, **hints)\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/core/numbers.py:Exp1._eval_rewrite_as_sin`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3724 | def _eval_rewrite_as_sin(self, **kwargs):\n3725 |         from sympy.functions.elementary.trigonometric import sin\n3726 |         return sin(I + S.Pi/2) - I*sin(I)\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/core/numbers.py:Exp1._eval_rewrite_as_cos`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3728 | def _eval_rewrite_as_cos(self, **kwargs):\n3729 |         from sympy.functions.elementary.trigonometric import cos\n3730 |         return cos(I) + I*cos(I + S.Pi/2)\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/core/numbers.py:Catalan._eval_rewrite_as_Sum`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n4049 | def _eval_rewrite_as_Sum(self, k_sym=None, symbols=None, **hints):\n4050 |         if (k_sym is not None) or (symbols is not None):\n4051 |             return self\n4052 |         from .symbol import Dummy\n4053 |         from sympy.concrete.summations import Sum\n4054 |         k = Dummy('k', integer=True, nonnegative=True)\n4055 |         return Sum(S.NegativeOne**k / (2*k+1)**2, (k, 0, S.Infinity))\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:dpll_int_repr`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 98 | def dpll_int_repr(clauses, symbols, model):\n 99 |     \"\"\"\n100 |     Compute satisfiability in a partial model.\n101 |     Arguments are expected to be in integer representation\n102 | \n103 |     >>> from sympy.logic.algorithms.dpll import dpll_int_repr\n104 |     >>> dpll_int_repr([{1}, {2}, {3}], {1, 2}, {3: False})\n105 |     False\n106 | \n107 |     \"\"\"\n108 |     # compute DP kernel\n109 |     P, value = find_unit_clause_int_repr(clauses, model)\n110 |     while P:\n111 |         model.update({P: value})\n112 |         symbols.remove(P)\n113 |         if not value:\n114 |             P = -P\n115 |         clauses = unit_propagate_int_repr(clauses, P)\n116 |         P, value = find_unit_clause_int_repr(clauses, model)\n117 |     P, value = find_pure_symbol_int_repr(symbols, clauses)\n118 |     while P:\n119 |         model.update({P: value})\n120 |         symbols.remove(P)\n121 |         if not value:\n122 |             P = -P\n123 |         clauses = unit_propagate_int_repr(clauses, P)\n124 |         P, value = find_pure_symbol_int_repr(symbols, clauses)\n125 |     # end DP kernel\n126 |     unknown_clauses = []\n127 |     for c in clauses:\n128 |         val = pl_true_int_repr(c, model)\n129 |         if val is False:\n130 |             return False\n131 |         if val is not True:\n132 |             unknown_clauses.append(c)\n133 |     if not unknown_clauses:\n134 |         return model\n135 |     P = symbols.pop()\n136 |     model_copy = model.copy()\n137 |     model.update({P: True})\n138 |     model_copy.update({P: False})\n139 |     symbols_copy = symbols.copy()\n140 |     return (dpll_int_repr(unit_propagate_int_repr(unknown_clauses, P), symbols, model) or\n141 |             dpll_int_repr(unit_propagate_int_repr(unknown_clauses, -P), symbols_copy, model_copy))\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:pl_true_int_repr`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n146 | def pl_true_int_repr(clause, model={}):\n147 |     \"\"\"\n148 |     Lightweight version of pl_true.\n149 |     Argument clause represents the set of args of an Or clause. This is used\n150 |     inside dpll_int_repr, it is not meant to be used directly.\n151 | \n152 |     >>> from sympy.logic.algorithms.dpll import pl_true_int_repr\n153 |     >>> pl_true_int_repr({1, 2}, {1: False})\n154 |     >>> pl_true_int_repr({1, 2}, {1: False, 2: False})\n155 |     False\n156 | \n157 |     \"\"\"\n158 |     result = False\n159 |     for lit in clause:\n160 |         if lit < 0:\n161 |             p = model.get(-lit)\n162 |             if p is not None:\n163 |                 p = not p\n164 |         else:\n165 |             p = model.get(lit)\n166 |         if p is True:\n167 |             return True\n168 |         elif p is None:\n169 |             result = None\n170 |     return result\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/logic/algorithms/dpll.py:unit_propagate`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n173 | def unit_propagate(clauses, symbol):\n174 |     \"\"\"\n175 |     Returns an equivalent set of clauses\n176 |     If a set of clauses contains the unit clause l, the other clauses are\n177 |     simplified by the application of the two following rules:\n178 | \n179 |       1. every clause containing l is removed\n180 |       2. in every clause that contains ~l this literal is deleted\n181 | \n182 |     Arguments are expected to be in CNF.\n183 | \n184 |     >>> from sympy.abc import A, B, D\n185 |     >>> from sympy.logic.algorithms.dpll import unit_propagate\n186 |     >>> unit_propagate([A | B, D | ~B, B], B)\n187 |     [D, B]\n188 | \n189 |     \"\"\"\n190 |     output = []\n191 |     for c in clauses:\n192 |         if c.func != Or:\n193 |             output.append(c)\n194 |             continue\n195 |         for arg in c.args:\n196 |             if arg == ~symbol:\n197 |                 output.append(Or(*[x for x in c.args if x != ~symbol]))\n198 |                 break\n199 |             if arg == symbol:\n200 |                 break\n201 |         else:\n202 |             output.append(c)\n203 |     return output\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the search results, it's clear that the `_eval_rewrite_as_` methods are typically defined in classes that extend `Expr` or other classes in SymPy. Given that the `sign` function's behavior is described in the issue, we should look into the `sign` function's implementation to see where it calls `_eval_rewrite_as_`.\n\nLet's search for the `sign` function's implementation.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['sign']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>'**/*.py'</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern ''**/*.py''. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"sign\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `sign`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass sign(DefinedFunction):\n\n    is_complex = True\n    _singularities = True\n\n    def doit(self, **hints):\n...\n    @classmethod\n    def eval(cls, arg):\n...\n    def _eval_Abs(self):\n...\n    def _eval_conjugate(self):\n...\n    def _eval_derivative(self, x):\n...\n    def _eval_is_nonnegative(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_nonpositive(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_imaginary(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_integer(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_zero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_power(self, other):\n...\n    def _eval_nseries(self, x, n, logx, cdir=0):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Piecewise(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Heaviside(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Abs(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_simplify(self, **kwargs):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sympy/series/gruntz.py:sign`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `sign`.\n```\n368 | def sign(e, x):\n369 |     \"\"\"\n370 |     Returns a sign of an expression e(x) for x->oo.\n371 | \n372 |     ::\n373 | \n374 |         e >  0 for x sufficiently large ...  1\n375 |         e == 0 for x sufficiently large ...  0\n376 |         e <  0 for x sufficiently large ... -1\n377 | \n378 |     The result of this function is currently undefined if e changes sign\n379 |     arbitrarily often for arbitrarily large x (e.g. sin(x)).\n380 | \n381 |     Note that this returns zero only if e is *constantly* zero\n382 |     for x sufficiently large. [If e is constant, of course, this is just\n383 |     the same thing as the sign of e.]\n384 |     \"\"\"\n385 |     if not isinstance(e, Basic):\n386 |         raise TypeError(\"e should be an instance of Basic\")\n387 | \n388 |     if e.is_positive:\n389 |         return 1\n390 |     elif e.is_negative:\n391 |         return -1\n392 |     elif e.is_zero:\n393 |         return 0\n394 | \n395 |     elif not e.has(x):\n396 |         from sympy.simplify import logcombine\n397 |         e = logcombine(e)\n398 |         return _sign(e)\n399 |     elif e == x:\n400 |         return 1\n401 |     elif e.is_Mul:\n402 |         a, b = e.as_two_terms()\n403 |         sa = sign(a, x)\n404 |         if not sa:\n405 |             return 0\n406 |         return sa * sign(b, x)\n407 |     elif isinstance(e, exp):\n408 |         return 1\n409 |     elif e.is_Pow:\n410 |         if e.base == S.Exp1:\n411 |             return 1\n412 |         s = sign(e.base, x)\n413 |         if s == 1:\n414 |             return 1\n415 |         if e.exp.is_Integer:\n416 |             return s**e.exp\n417 |     elif isinstance(e, log) and e.args[0].is_positive:\n418 |         return sign(e.args[0] - 1, x)\n419 | \n420 |     # if all else fails, do it the hard way\n421 |     c0, e0 = mrv_leadterm(e, x)\n422 |     return sign(c0, x)\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/physics/control/lti.py:Feedback.sign`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `sign`.\n```\n2861 | def sign(self):\n2862 |         \"\"\"\n2863 |         Returns the type of MIMO Feedback model. ``1``\n2864 |         for Positive and ``-1`` for Negative.\n2865 |         \"\"\"\n2866 |         return self.args[2]\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/physics/control/lti.py:MIMOFeedback.sign`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `sign`.\n```\n3318 | def sign(self):\n3319 |         r\"\"\"\n3320 |         Returns the type of feedback interconnection of two models. ``1``\n3321 |         for Positive and ``-1`` for Negative.\n3322 |         \"\"\"\n3323 |         return self.args[2]\n```\n"}, {"content": "The most relevant result is the `sign` class defined in `sympy/functions/elementary/co